  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Boletim de Urna",
    "overview": "Every figure on the site is printed as the receipt a Brazilian ballot box prints when the polls close, summed municipality by municipality. The ground is cool thermal paper laid on a slightly greyer counter. The ink is thermal black that fades to grey for anything secondary. Only two coloured inks exist: thermal red for the left and carbon-copy blue for the right. Numbers, labels, headers and controls are set in a monospace receipt face. Sentences use a hyperlegible companion face. The system rejects the dark dashboard of KPI tiles and the cream broadsheet.\n\nDensity is that of a printed slip: compact rows with tabular figures, hairline and dashed rules, and no ornament that a thermal printer could not make. State is printed as a mark, never as a colour. A provisional result gets a rubber stamp, imputed data gets a stamp, a municipality with too few votes is hatched, missing data is left blank, and a selection is bracketed, underlined with a wave or punched. Sections are separated by perforated tear lines. A section carries at most one monumental numeral and highlights the one line that matters.\n\nThe world has a dark theme (\"carbon\"), a more-contrast context and a reduced-motion context, all driven by the same tokens. The diverging map ramp is read from the tokens at runtime, so the map re-inks when the theme changes.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Thermal paper on a counter: flat tonal layers, torn zigzag edges, dashed perforations.",
      "Hue is reserved for ideology. Red means left, blue means right, and the centre of the scale is paper grey.",
      "State is a mark: stamps, brackets, punched holes, hatching, blanks.",
      "Martian Mono prints every number and label. Atkinson Hyperlegible Next carries every sentence.",
      "At most one monumental numeral per section, in light-weight mono at up to 6rem."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Two Inks Rule",
        "body": "Hue means ideology and nothing else. Thermal red is left and carbon-copy blue is right, on a diverging scale that passes through paper grey. Gender, office, arena, selection and status never get a hue.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The State Is a Mark Rule",
        "body": "A data state is printed, never coloured. Provisional and imputed data get a stamp. Few votes gets hatching. Missing data is blank paper grey. A selection is shown with a bracket, a wavy underline, a 2px ink border or a punched hole.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Ink Is the Accent Rule",
        "body": "Links, focus rings, active tabs and pressed buttons use thermal ink. The kit's status reds, greens and ambers are not part of this world.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Receipt Type Rule",
        "body": "If the machine would print it (a number, a code, a label, a header, a control), it is set in Martian Mono. If a person would say it as a sentence, it is set in Atkinson. Uppercase is allowed only in mono labels, page titles and stamps (a registered exception in PRODUCT.md), never in running prose.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Numeral Rule",
        "body": "Each section carries at most one monumental numeral. It is the reading that matters. Every other number stays at receipt size.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Figures Rule",
        "body": "Every number in a row, table, list, ranking or readout uses tabular lining figures, so the columns line up like a printed tally.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Paper-on-Counter Rule",
        "body": "Something sits above the page only because it is a separate slip of paper, never because it floats. Lift is shown with tone and torn edges, never with blur.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do print every number, code and label in Martian Mono, with tabular lining figures wherever numbers sit in rows.",
      "Do mark data state with a printed mark: a −4° double-ruled stamp for provisional, imputed or experimental data, 45° hatching (1px lines at 4px pitch) for fewer than 300 female votes, and blank paper grey for missing data.",
      "Do separate sections with a perforated tear line (2px dashed `paper-tear`, `spacing.xl` above and below).",
      "Do give a section at most one monumental numeral (Martian Mono 300, up to 6rem, −0.04em) with an uppercase mono label above it.",
      "Do draw ideology on the diverging scale from `ideo-left-strong` through `ideo-center` to `ideo-right-strong`, reading the colours from the tokens at runtime so the dark theme re-inks the map.",
      "Do mark the current or selected item with a bracket, a wavy underline, a 2px ink border or a punched hole.",
      "Do encode arena and average by stroke and fill: solid versus dashed lines, filled dot for the typical municipality, hollow ring for the electorate."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't use red or blue for anything other than left and right. That rules out women versus men, mayor versus governor, selection markers and status messages.",
      "Don't add box-shadows, floating cards, or corners rounder than 4px on surfaces and controls. Paper is square and lies flat.",
      "Don't set running prose in uppercase, and don't set long-form prose (prose blocks, ledes, narrative steps) in mono. Uppercase is limited to mono labels, page titles and stamps.",
